/* book: lead: A specific heat is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of a gas by 1 K. At constant volume the heat goes only into internal energy, so q = cvΔT. At constant pressure the gas also expands and does work, so q = cpΔT and cp is larger. For an ideal gas cp − cv = R. Only a few universities teach this as a separate topic, but these numbers size compressors, engine cylinders and air-conditioning coils. */
/* book Figure 2.3.1: Warming 2 kg of air by 20 K. In the rigid tank all 28.72 kJ of heat (mcvΔT) raises U. In the cylinder the same rise in U needs 40.20 kJ (mcpΔT), because 11.48 kJ (mRΔT) leaves as work on the piston. */
