/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - cv = (∂u/∂T)v, cp = (∂h/∂T)p
   - u and h are the specific internal energy and enthalpy (kJ/kg); T is the temperature (K); the subscript outside the bracket is the property held fixed.
   - For an ideal gas u and h depend only on T, so du = cv dT and dh = cp dT along any path, not only at constant volume or pressure.
   - For 1 kg of ideal gas at constant pressure, the work is pΔv = RΔT.
   - cp − cv = R, γ = cp/cv
   - R is the gas constant of the gas (kJ/kg·K), equal to the universal gas constant Ru = 8.314 kJ/kmol·K divided by the molar mass M (kg/kmol); γ is the specific-heat ratio, a pure number.
   - cv = R/(γ − 1), cp = γR/(γ − 1)
   - obtained by solving the two equations above together; useful when a question gives only R and γ.
   - Typical values: monatomic gases such as helium and argon γ ≈ 1.67; diatomic gases such as air, N₂ and O₂ γ ≈ 1.4; polyatomic gases such as CO₂ and steam γ ≈ 1.3.
*/
