/* book Worked example: Q: 1 kg of air at 300 K and 100 kPa is compressed to one-eighth of its volume. Find the final pressure, final temperature, work and heat if the process is (a) isothermal and (b) reversible adiabatic. Take R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K, cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4.
   1. Data: m = 1 kg, T₁ = 300 K, p₁ = 100 kPa, V₁/V₂ = 8. V₁ = mRT₁/p₁ = 0.287 × 300/100 = 0.861 m³, so V₂ = 0.1076 m³.
   2. (a) Isothermal: T₂ = 300 K and p₂ = p₁ × 8 = 800 kPa.
   3. W = mRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 0.287 × 300 × ln(1/8) = 86.1 × (−2.0794) = −179.0 kJ.
   4. ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas at constant T, so Q = W = −179.0 kJ: 179.0 kJ of heat leaves.
   5. (b) Adiabatic: p₂ = p₁(V₁/V₂)γ = 100 × 81.4 = 100 × 18.379 = 1838 kPa.
   6. T₂ = T₁(V₁/V₂)γ−1 = 300 × 80.4 = 300 × 2.2974 = 689.2 K.
   7. W = mcv(T₁ − T₂) = 0.718 × (300 − 689.2) = 0.718 × (−389.2) = −279.5 kJ; Q = 0, so ΔU = +279.5 kJ.
   Answer: Isothermal: p₂ = 800 kPa, T₂ = 300 K, W = Q = −179.0 kJ. Adiabatic: p₂ = 1838 kPa, T₂ = 689.2 K, W = −279.5 kJ, Q = 0. To the same final volume the adiabatic needs more work because the gas heats up and its pressure climbs faster. */
