/* book exam traps: Putting n = 1 into (p₁V₁ − p₂V₂)/(n − 1): it divides by zero. Use the ln formula. | Using γ when the question gives n. A path with n = 1.3 is not adiabatic even though air has γ = 1.4. | Thinking a gas that gets hotter must be receiving heat. With 1 < n < γ the gas heats up and rejects heat, as the second example shows. | For an ideal gas, isothermal means ΔU = 0, not Q = 0; adiabatic means Q = 0, not ΔT = 0. | Comparing paths without saying what is fixed. To the same final volume adiabatic compression needs more work than isothermal (279.5 against 179.0 kJ above); to the same final pressure a closed-system adiabatic needs less, because the volume falls less. */
